[38;5;231m⏺[39m Got it — general-purpose, no named consumers yet. I'll design for extensibility but resist building rules nobody has
  asked for.

  Next fork, and it's the one that decides how much reuse you actually get.

  Right now [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m does one thing: check a plain object of values. But the [3mrepeated[0m work in a form isn't the [38;5;153mif[39m
  statement — it's everything around it: reading values out of the DOM, running the rules, and putting error messages on
  screen. How much of that the shared layer owns determines whether form #2 is ten lines or sixty.

  - [1mRules only[0m — a pure [38;5;153mvalidate(values, schema)[39m returning errors. Trivial to test, zero DOM coupling, works in Node.
    Cost: every new form still hand-writes its own [38;5;153mgetElementById[39m reads and its own error display, so the copy-paste
    you're trying to kill mostly survives.
  - [1mRules + DOM binding[0m — the above, plus an [38;5;153mattach(formEl, schema, onValid)[39m that reads fields by [38;5;153mname[39m, runs validation
    on submit, and renders messages next to each input. A new form becomes a schema plus one call. Cost: the layer now
    owns markup conventions and error-element structure; changing that later touches every form.
  - [1mRules + binding + live feedback[0m — adds blur/input revalidation and dirty-field tracking. Better UX, but it's real
    state management, and it's speculative when no second form exists yet.

  Cheap to change later: adding rule types, adding live feedback on top of a binding layer. Expensive to change later:
  the schema format itself and the error-markup convention, since every form encodes them.

  My recommendation is the middle option, with the two halves kept as separate modules so the pure validator stays
  usable and testable on its own.
